            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 559)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Fixed: pasting a review link could say "didn't parse".</b> Asobi hands out three shapes of link and could only read two of them — the long form, which is what every link falls back to, was the one it didn't know. All three work now, and a link that names you gives you your name, your watermark and your notes attributed to you.</li>
<li><b>Deliver a session through the studio instead of the internet.</b> When a studio watcher is configured, the Deliver sheet offers it: the bundle is staged on inari under <code>/mnt/production_data</code> rather than going out through the review link, and it's swept after its days are up. Reachable from the studio network only — which is what it's for. Everything else about the delivery is the same, including the link, the room and the notes coming back.</li>
<li><b>The Shared Review window says which way in is which.</b> Two buttons both called "Join" have become <b>Someone sent you a link</b> — the common case, and how a delivered session arrives — and <b>Someone is hosting nearby</b>, for the same network or tailnet with a passcode.</li>
<li><b>The web review carries the Autonomy mark</b> in its header, sitting on the line with the guide and the clock.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
